Quite nice

The remainder of the manuscript is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:methodology} develops the methodology: the MAP prior, the average metrics with their closed forms under Normal priors, the extension of the EUII to the borrowing framework, the Dual Criterion, and group sequential designs. Section~\ref{sec:Applications} applies the framework to a case study in Crohn's disease, evaluating fixed and sequential designs under correctly specified and misspecified design priors. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes with a discussion of the findings and directions for future work.

\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}
For the remainder of the manuscript, we focus on two-sample clinical trials with a continuous Normal endpoint and known standard deviations $\sigma_C$ and $\sigma_T$. 
Let $\theta_T$ and $\theta_C$ denote the true parameters of the treatment and control groups, respectively. The treatment contrast is defined as $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$,
where we assume without loss of generality that lower values of $\theta$ represent a better clinical response, thus $\delta > 0$ indicates a treatment benefit. 
 
We evaluate the one-sided hypothesis test:
$$
H_0: \delta \le q \quad \text{versus} \quad H_1: \delta > q,
$$

where $q$ denotes the reference value defining the null hypothesis, for instance $q = 0$ for a superiority test or a non-inferiority margin $q < 0$.

In the Bayesian framework, the trial design evaluates this hypothesis using a posterior decision rule.
Let $\mathcal{D} = (\bar{y}_T, \bar{y}_C)$ denote the observed trial data.
The trial is declared a success (i.e., $H_0$ is rejected) if the posterior probability of $\delta$ exceeding $q$ surpasses a pre-specified threshold $p$ (typically $p = 1 - \alpha$):
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:an_prior}
    \Pr(\delta > q \mid \mathcal{D},\, \pi_A) \ge p,
\end{equation}
where $\pi_A$ explicitly denotes the analysis prior used to derive the posterior distribution.  

\subsection{Running example}
To fix ideas, throughout this section we illustrate all results using a Running Example: the same Crohn's disease trial developed in full in Section~\ref{sec:application1}.
It is a two-arm trial: $n_T = 40$ treatment patients, $n_C = 20$ control patients, and
known standard deviation $\sigma = 88$ (lower values are better), giving sampling variances
$v_C = 88^2/20$ and $v_T = 88^2/40$.
Success is declared when $\Pr(\delta > 0 \mid \mathcal{D},\,\pi_A) \ge 0.95$. The analysis
prior $\pi_A(\theta_C)$ is derived in Section~\ref{subsec:formulating_priors} from six historical
placebo arms.

\subsection{Formulating the MAP Prior}
\label{subsec:formulating_priors}
The Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) prior is derived from a hierarchical model that uses the exchangeability assumption across
historical trials \citep{schmidli_robust_2014}. 

\subsubsection{Meta-Analytic-Predictive prior}
Let $H_{\text{hist}}$ denote the number of historical studies, with observed
control mean $\bar{y}_{h}$ and known sampling variances $v_h = \sigma^2/n_h$ for $h = 1,\ldots,H_{\text{hist}}$. The model is:
\begin{align*}
    \bar{y}_h \mid \theta_h &\sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_h,\, v_h) \\
    \theta_h,\, \theta_C \mid \mu, \tau &\sim \mathcal{N}(\mu,\, \tau^2),
\end{align*}
with hyperpriors $p(\mu)$ and $p(\tau)$ placed on the population mean and between-study standard deviation. The heterogeneity
parameter $\tau$ governs the degree of borrowing: $\tau \approx 0$ implies all studies share a common control mean (full pooling),
while large $\tau$ means large between-trial variation and justifies reduced borrowing.

The MAP prior for the new trial's control parameter $\theta_C$ is the marginal posterior predictive distribution given the historical data:
\begin{equation*} 
p\!\left(\theta_C \mid \bar{y}_1,\ldots,\bar{y}_{H_{\text{hist}}}\right)
    \propto \int\!\int p\!\left(\theta_C \mid \mu,\tau\right)
    \left[\prod_{h=1}^{H_{\text{hist}}} \int p(\bar{y}_h \mid \theta_h)\, p(\theta_h \mid \mu,\tau)\, d\theta_h\right]
    p(\mu)\,p(\tau)\; d\mu\; d\tau.
\end{equation*}
This integral is not available in closed form and is evaluated via MCMC. The resulting posterior samples of $\theta_C$ are
then used to fit a $K$-component mixture of Normal distributions:
\begin{equation*}
    \pi_{\mathrm{MAP}}(\theta_C) = \sum_{k=1}^{K} w_k\,\mathcal{N}(\theta_C;\,\mu_k,\,\sigma_k^2),
\end{equation*}
chosen by minimizing the KL divergence of the mixture from the true MAP predictive. This conjugate mixture
approximation enables closed-form Bayesian updating: given the current trial mean $\bar{y}_C \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_C, v_C)$, each
Normal component is updated independently via the standard Normal-Normal formula, and the posterior is again a Normal mixture with adjusted weights.

\subsubsection{Robust MAP prior}
The Robust MAP prior augments $\pi_{\mathrm{MAP}}$ with a weakly informative component to protect against prior-data conflict:
\begin{equation*}
    \pi_{\mathrm{Robust}}(\theta_C)
    = (1 - \omega)\,\pi_{\mathrm{MAP}}(\theta_C) + \omega\,\mathcal{N}(\theta_C;\,\mu_R,\,\sigma_R^2),
\end{equation*}
where $\omega \in [0,1]$ is the prior probability that the new trial is not exchangeable with the historical controls, and $\mu_R$,
$\sigma_R^2$ are the location and variance of the vague component.
The borrowing is dynamic because the mixture weights are themselves updated by the data. After observing the control data $\bar{y}_C$, the posterior weight on the vague component becomes \citep{neuenschwander_fixed_2023}:
\begin{equation*}
    \tilde{\omega} = \frac{\omega\, m_R(\bar{y}_C)}{\omega\, m_R(\bar{y}_C) + (1 - \omega)\, m_{\mathrm{MAP}}(\bar{y}_C)},
\end{equation*}
where $m_j(\bar{y}_C) = \int \pi_j(\theta_C)\, f(\bar{y}_C \mid \theta_C)\, d\theta_C$, for $j \in \{\mathrm{MAP},\, R\}$, is the marginal likelihood of the data under each component. Under prior-data conflict the vague component explains the observed data better, $m_R(\bar{y}_C) > m_{\mathrm{MAP}}(\bar{y}_C)$, so $\tilde{\omega} > \omega$ and the posterior down weights the historical component. The design therefore borrows most when prior and data agree and least when they conflict, limiting the influence of misleading historical information.
Following \citet{schmidli_robust_2014}, throughout this thesis we set the vague component to a unit-information prior (UIP): a Normal with variance $\sigma_R^2 = \sigma^2$ and $\mu_R$ equal to the historical mean, which is weakly informative yet proper.

The tuning of $\omega$, $\mu_R$ and $\sigma_R^2$ is not trivial in general:
\citet{ratta2026interplay} show that Lindley's paradox (that is, even though a very dispersed robust component might not seem influential, it will result in a high posterior weight being assigned to the informative component) and asymptotic T1E inflation can be avoided only when $\omega$ and $\sigma_R^2$
are calibrated jointly rather than independently. Instead, \citet{weru_information_2026} demonstrate that a heavier-tailed vague component or a data-dependent location can more
effectively cap T1E rates under severe drift. Regarding $\omega$ itself, \citet{neuenschwander_fixed_2023}
argue that it does not need to be assigned a hyperprior: even a fixed weight is implicitly updated by the current data through the mixture
posterior weights, so a Beta prior on $\omega$ adds little beyond additional complexity.


\paragraph{Running example.}
To obtain the MAP prior, we follow the worked example at \url{https://opensource.nibr.com/RBesT/articles/introduction_normal.html}. 
Particularly, we fit the hierarchical model via MCMC by assuming a weakly informative Normal on $\mu$ centred at zero with scale $\sigma$, and a
HalfNormal on $\tau$ with scale $\sigma/2$, a weakly informative choice that places most prior mass on small between-trial heterogeneity. Then, the \texttt{RBesT} package exploits EM algorithm to select the number of components $K$ using AIC.

Applying these steps to the six historical placebo arms yields the hierarchical model fit shown in Figure~\ref{fig:map_forest}, and Figure~\ref{fig:map_robust_plot} compares the resulting single-component Normal approximation with the 3-component mixture selected by AIC and its robustified version. 
Each prior is summarised by its effective sample size (ESS), roughly the number of concurrent control patients it is worth \citep{neuenschwander2020predictively}. Because the ESS depends on the shape of the approximating prior, the single Normal, the mixture and the robust mixture summarise the same MAP predictive with different values (respectively $20$, $40$ and $29$). 
Throughout this section we adopt the single-component Normal as the analysis prior of the running example, which allows us to obtain closed form results. The full 3-component mixture and its robust extension are inspected in the Application in Section~\ref{sec:Applications}.
<<map_setup, include=FALSE, cache=TRUE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>=
library(RBesT)
library(ggplot2)
crohn_sigma <- 88
dat <- crohn
dat$y.se <- crohn_sigma / sqrt(dat$n)
set.seed(1)
map_mcmc <- gMAP(cbind(y, y.se) ~ 1 | study,
  weights = n, data = dat,
  family = gaussian,
  beta.prior = cbind(0, crohn_sigma),
  tau.dist = "HalfNormal", tau.prior = cbind(0, crohn_sigma / 2)
)
map_fit_1 <- automixfit(map_mcmc, Nc = 1)   # single Normal
map_fit_3 <- automixfit(map_mcmc)            # full mixture: selected by AIC
ess_map_1 <- round(ess(map_fit_1))
ess_map_3 <- round(ess(map_fit_3))

map_robust <- robustify(map_fit_3, weight = 0.2, mean = -50, sigma = crohn_sigma)
ess_map_rob <- round(ess(map_robust))

@

<<map_forest, echo=FALSE, cache=TRUE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=8, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Forest plot of the hierarchical meta-analysis fit. For each historical placebo arm, the observed control mean $\\bar{y}_{C,h}$ with 95\\% interval is shown alongside the posterior mean and 95\\% credible interval of $\\theta_h$. The bottom row (\\textit{MAP}) is the posterior predictive for the new trial's $\\theta_C$.">>=
forest_plot(
  map_mcmc,
  prob       = 0.95,      # 95% credible intervals
  est        = "MAP",    # show only the MAP predictive row (theta_C)
  model      = "both",  # show both raw (stratified) and shrunken (meta) per-study estimates
  point_est  = "median",  # posterior median as point estimate
  size       = 1.25,
  linewidth  = 1.25
) + ylab(expression(theta[C])) #for some reason they made the plot inverted x and y axis 
@

<<map_robust_plot, echo=FALSE, cache=TRUE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=8, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap=paste0("MAP prior approximations and robust extension. The 1-component Normal (ESS $= ", ess_map_1, "$, used in the running example) and the 3-component mixture selected by AIC (ESS $= ", ess_map_3, "$) both approximate $\\pi_{\\mathrm{MAP}}$. Adding a weakly informative UIP component with weight $\\omega = 0.2$ yields the Robust MAP (ESS $= ", ess_map_rob, "$).")>>=
theta_grid <- seq(-300, 180, length.out = 600)
labels <- c("1-component MAP", "3-component MAP", "Robust MAP")
# legend labels as plotmath so that omega renders as the symbol
legend_labels <- c(
  "1-component MAP",
  "3-component MAP",
  "Robust MAP"
)
df_map <- rbind(
  data.frame(theta = theta_grid, density = dmix(map_fit_1,  theta_grid), prior = labels[1]),
  data.frame(theta = theta_grid, density = dmix(map_fit_3,  theta_grid), prior = labels[2]),
  data.frame(theta = theta_grid, density = dmix(map_robust, theta_grid), prior = labels[3])
)
df_map$prior <- factor(df_map$prior, levels = labels)
ggplot(df_map, aes(x = theta, y = density, colour = prior)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.9) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c("#0072B2", "#D55E00", "#009E73"), labels = legend_labels) +
  labs(x = expression(theta[C]), y = "Density", colour = NULL, linetype = NULL) +
  coord_cartesian(xlim = c(-150, 70)) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom")
@




\subsection{Metrics to Evaluate Bayesian Designs}
\label{subsec:metrics_bayesian}
Frequentist T1E and Power are well-established metrics used to evaluate traditional trial designs. However, it is documented that when informative priors are incorporated into a 
design, strict point-wise control of the frequentist T1E is impossible \citep{viele_use_2014, kopp2024simulating}. Consequently, in the Bayesian framework, alternative metrics are often evaluated, such 
as Assurance \citep{ohagan_assurance_2005} or Predictive Power \citep{micheloud_power_2022, spiegelhalter_monitoring_1986}.



All these metrics are special cases of a common metric $m$ \citep{best_beyond_2025}, which can be expressed as the expected probability of study success, integrated over a 
specified prior distribution $\pi_D$ for a parameter $\theta$:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:m_general}
m = \int \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta\right) \pi_D(\theta) \, d\theta.
\end{equation}


\subsubsection{Design Prior versus Analysis Prior}
It is crucial to differentiate between design and analysis prior \citep{ohagan_bayesian_2001}.  
\begin{itemize}
  \item The analysis prior $\pi_A(\theta)$ is the distribution used during the actual analysis of the trial, to derive the posterior and evaluate the decision rule (Equation~\eqref{eq:an_prior}). In this manuscript, 
$\pi_A(\theta)$ incorporates historical data via a Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) prior, but other methods can be employed (see Section~\ref{subsec:info-borrowing})
  \item  The design prior $\pi_D(\theta)$, (see Equation~\eqref{eq:m_general}) is the assumed distribution of the underlying true parameter (data generating process) used exclusively in the planning stage to evaluate the design's 
operating characteristics across various plausible true scenarios.
\end{itemize}
While the approach of using the exact same prior beliefs for both the data generation and the inference ($\pi_D = \pi_A$) 
is considered the most logically consistent and natural by many Bayesian 
methodologists \citep{ohagan_bayesian_2001}, it may be not sufficient to regulatory authorities. In fact, a more comprehensive sensitivity analysis can be required, in order to evaluate the 
design under "prior-data conflict" or "drift" scenarios, where the prior information used for analysis ($\pi_A$) differs from the true data generating process ($\pi_D$). 

Historical information can be borrowed by placing a prior either on the control group $\theta_C$ (known in the literature as hybrid-control trials) or directly on the treatment contrast $\delta$ \citep{best_beyond_2025}. In this thesis, we focus on borrowing on the control group. Section~\ref{subsec:contrast_limitation} explains why the metrics developed below, do not extend straightforwardly to the treatment contrast.




\subsubsection{Average Metrics}
\phantomsection\label{sec:pointwise}
When historical borrowing is applied on the control arm, the metrics depend jointly on the true parameters $\theta_C$ and $\theta_T$. The general metric from Equation~\eqref{eq:m_general} expands
 to a double integral over the joint design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C, \theta_T)$:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:m_joint}
m = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T\right) \pi_D(\theta_C, \theta_T) \, d\theta_T \, d\theta_C.
\end{equation}
The traditional definitions of both T1E and Power impose deterministic constraints 
on the relationship between the treatment and control parameters. Specifically, we can fix the treatment 
contrast at a constant value $\delta$, such that $\theta_T = \theta_C - \delta$. Therefore, the joint design prior is reduced to a marginal prior on the control group, 
$\pi_D(\theta_C)$, collapsing Equation~\ref{eq:m_joint} into a single integral, and by varying the assumed true effect size $\delta$, we can define two metrics for evaluating the trial design:

\paragraph{Average T1E (avgT1E)}
When the true treatment effect is null ($\delta = 0$), we obtain the Average T1E \citep{best_beyond_2025}:  
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:avg-type1-error}
  \avgTIE = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C\right) \pi_D(\theta_C) \, d\theta_C.
\end{equation}
\Remark By defining $\pi_D(\theta_C)$ as a Dirac delta function centered at a specific value $\theta_C^*$, Equation~\ref{eq:avg-type1-error} collapses 
to the classic frequentist T1E at $\theta_C^*$.  It is easy to notice that, in this case,
the conditional T1E is not a single constant, but varies pointwise as a function of $\theta_C^*$. 


\paragraph{Average Power (avgPower)}
When the true treatment effect is assumed to be a specific non-zero value ($\delta \neq 0$),  we obtain the Average Power (at $\delta$):  
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avg-power}
  \avgPow(\delta) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C - \delta\right) \pi_D(\theta_C) \, d\theta_C.
\end{equation}
\Remark We use the term "Average Power" rather than "Assurance" for Equation~\ref{eq:avg-power} to avoid confusion. Assurance often 
implies marginalization over a prior distribution for the effect size $\delta$ \citep{ohagan_assurance_2005}. Because our framework fixes 
$\delta$ and integrates solely over the control parameter space $\pi_D(\theta_C)$, "Average Power" prevents ambiguity.


%% As an alternative, it is also proposed to compute the maximum (or minimum) operating characteristic over a plausible subset of the parameter space, 
%% denoted as $\Theta_C$. Then this restricted maximum T1E is expressed as:
%% $$\max_{\theta_C \in \Theta_C} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C - \delta\right)$$

\subsubsection{Closed Forms under Normal Priors}

Assuming Normal distributions for both the historical analysis prior ($\pi_A$) and the design prior ($\pi_D$), it is possible to derive closed-form solutions for Equation~\eqref{eq:avg-power} and ~\eqref{eq:avg-type1-error}. 
Complete proofs are reported in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_normal_priors}. 

Let the observed sample means for the control and treatment arms be normally distributed as $\bar{y}_C \mid \theta_C \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_C, v_C)$ and $\bar{y}_T \mid \theta_T \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_T, v_T)$, respectively, with $v_{\bullet} = \sigma_{\bullet}^{2}/n_{\bullet}$. 
We incorporate an informative historical analysis prior for the control arm, $\pi_A(\theta_C) \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_{A,C}, \sigma_{A,C}^2)$, where $\mu_{A,C}$ represents the historical mean of control group and $\sigma_{A,C}^2$ quantifies the prior uncertainty. We assume a non-informative analysis prior for the treatment arm.
The Bayesian Trial is declared a success if $\Pr(\delta > q \mid \bar{y}_C, \bar{y}_T) > p$, consistent with Equation~\eqref{eq:an_prior}.

Let $\hat{\delta}$ denote the posterior mean of the treatment contrast ($\delta$). By Normal-Normal conjugacy, the posterior mean of the control arm is a convex combination governed by $W = \sigma_{A,C}^2 / (\sigma_{A,C}^2 + v_C)$. Consequently, we can rewrite the condition of Success in the form: 

\begin{equation} \label{eq:threshold_k}
    \hat{\delta} > k \qquad \text{with } k = q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}},
\end{equation}
where $q$ is the target efficacy threshold, $z_p$ is the Normal quantile corresponding to the required posterior probability, and $V_{\delta} = \sigma_{A,C}^2 v_C / (\sigma_{A,C}^2 + v_C) + v_T$ is the posterior variance of $\delta$.

\paragraph{Conditional Metrics}

By treating the true parameters as fixed constants ($\theta_C = \theta_C^*$ and $\theta_T = \theta_C^* - \delta$) and evaluating the frequentist sampling distribution of the estimator across repeated trials, we obtain the closed-form solutions for the conditional metrics. 

At a fixed $\theta_C$, the estimator $\hat{\delta}$ has sampling variance $V_{\text{samp}} = W^2 v_C + v_T$. This is distinct from the posterior variance $V_{\delta}$, which enters only through the decision threshold $z_p\sqrt{V_{\delta}}$. The Conditional Power, evaluated at a specific true control rate $\theta_C^*$ and true effect size $\delta$, is given by:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:power_closed_form}
    \Pow(\theta_C^*, \delta) = 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - \big[(1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \theta_C^*) + \delta\big]}{\sqrt{V_{\text{samp}}}} \right).
\end{equation}
The conditional T1E is simply the special case evaluated under the null hypothesis ($\delta = 0$):
\begin{equation} \label{eq:t1e_closed_form}
    \TIE(\theta_C^*) = 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - (1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \theta_C^*)}{\sqrt{V_{\text{samp}}}} \right).
\end{equation}
Note that as $W \to 1$ (a non-informative analysis prior) and under a common per patient variation $\sigma_C = \sigma_T = \sigma$, the bias term vanishes and the formulation collapses to the classical two sample $Z$-test power \citep{lachin1981introduction}:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:ztest_power}
    \Pow_Z(\delta) = 1 - \Phi\!\left( z_p - \frac{\delta - q}{\sigma\sqrt{\dfrac{1}{n_C} + \dfrac{1}{n_T}}} \right).
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Average Metrics}
 When using a design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C) \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_{D,C}, \sigma_{D,C}^2)$, we apply the Laws of Total Expectation and Total Variance to marginalize out the unknown parameter $\theta_C$. 

Marginalizing over $\pi_D$ inflates the estimator's variance to the marginal variance $V_{\text{marg}} = W^2 v_C + v_T + (1 - W)^2 \sigma_{D,C}^2 = V_{\text{samp}} + (1 - W)^2 \sigma_{D,C}^2$, which reduces to $V_{\text{samp}}$ when $\sigma_{D,C} \to 0$ (the conditional, or Dirac, case). The closed-form solution for the avgPower is:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avgpower_closed_form}
    \avgPow(\delta) = 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - \big[(1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \mu_{D,C}) + \delta\big]}{\sqrt{V_{\text{marg}}}} \right).
\end{equation}
Evaluating this distribution under the null hypothesis ($\delta = 0$) yields the avgT1E:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avgt1e_closed_form}
    \avgTIE = 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - (1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \mu_{D,C})}{\sqrt{V_{\text{marg}}}} \right).
\end{equation}
Consistent with the degenerate-prior view above, the conditional formulas of Equations~\eqref{eq:power_closed_form} and~\eqref{eq:t1e_closed_form} are exactly the limit of Equations~\eqref{eq:avgpower_closed_form} and~\eqref{eq:avgt1e_closed_form} as $\sigma_{D,C} \to 0$ with $\mu_{D,C} = \theta_C^*$: the term $(1 - W)^2 \sigma_{D,C}^2$ vanishes from the denominator and the design mean $\mu_{D,C}$ reduces to the fixed point $\theta_C^*$.

\paragraph{Observations on the Average Metrics}
\begin{itemize}
    \item  The denominator of the average metrics includes the term $(1 - W)^2 \sigma_{D,C}^2$. This accounts for the additional uncertainty of integrating over $\pi_D$. Because dividing by a larger variance
     shrinks a $Z$-score toward zero, the larger marginal variance forces average metrics closer to $50\%$ compared to conditional ones (evaluated at $\mu_{D,C}$).
    \item  When the analysis prior and design prior coincide ($\mu_{A,C} = \mu_{D,C}$ and $\sigma_{A,C}^2 = \sigma_{D,C}^2$) and the null threshold is $q = 0$, the bias term vanishes and, as shown in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_normal_priors}, the marginal variance equals the posterior variance ($V_{\text{marg}} = V_\delta$) and we obtain:
     \begin{align*}
          \avgPow(\delta) &= 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - \delta}{\sqrt{V_{\delta}}} \right) \\
          &= 1 - \Phi\left( z_p - \frac{\delta}{\sqrt{V_{\delta}}} \right),
     \end{align*} 
     thus, the avgT1E is controlled at exactly $1-p$, aligning with the literature \citep{best_beyond_2025}. 
    \item As already noted for the conditional metrics, $W \to 1$ collapses this formulation to the frequentist two sample Z test as well (Equation~\eqref{eq:ztest_power}).
    \item \citet{best_beyond_2025} evaluates the case of using a completely vague design prior. This leads the marginal variance in the denominator to diverge to infinity. Consequently, both the avgPower
    and the avgT1E converge to $\Phi(0) = 0.5$, no matter the choice of the analysis prior.
\end{itemize}



\paragraph{Running example.} 
In the figures below, the analysis prior is held fixed to the 1 component MAP prior ($\mu_{A,C} = -50$, $\sigma_{A,C}^2 = 88^2/20$) 
while the design prior parameters are varied to represent different assumptions about the true data generating process.

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_t1e} visualizes how T1E reacts to two types of conflict between analysis and design priors: location shift 
($\mu_{A,C} \neq \mu_{D,C}$) and scale shift ($\sigma_{A,C} \neq \sigma_{D,C}$). The left panel shows that if the true placebo 
response is better than expected ($\mu_{D,C} < -50$), the T1E increases. The conditional T1E (conditioned on $\theta_C^* = \mu_{D,C}$) 
lies below the avgT1E throughout the plotted range. In the right panel, when design uncertainty $\sigma_{D,C}$ is zero we obtain the 
conditional T1E, while as $\sigma_{D,C} \to \infty$ the avgT1E converges to $0.5$, consistent with Equation~\eqref{eq:avgt1e_closed_form}.

<<plot_t1e, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Left panel shows T1E under Location Shift (varying $\\mu_{D,C}$), assuming $\\sigma_{D,C} = \\sigma_{A,C}$. Conditional metric is evaluated at $\\theta_C^* = \\mu_{D,C}$. Right panel shows Average T1E under Scale Shift (varying $\\sigma_{D,C}$) while setting $\\mu_{D,C} = \\mu_{A,C}$. The vertical gray dotted lines indicate the points of prior alignment ($\\pi_D = \\pi_A$). The red dashed line represents the nominal value of $0.05$">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)
library(gridExtra)
library(scales)

# --- Trial Parameters  ---
sigma <- 88; n_C <- 20; n_T <- 40; n_A <- 20; N_total <- n_C + n_T
v_C <- sigma^2 / n_C; v_T <- sigma^2 / n_T; sigma_A2 <- sigma^2 / n_A; mu_A <- -50
q <- 0; z_p <- qnorm(0.95)
W <- sigma_A2 / (sigma_A2 + v_C)
V_delta <- W * v_C + v_T
V_samp <- W^2 * v_C + v_T
k_SC <- q + z_p * sqrt(V_delta)   # SC (significance) threshold

# rejection probabilities for a generic threshold k
# (SC: k = k_SC; dual criterion: k = max(DV, k_SC))
condPower <- function(theta_C_star, delta, k)
  1 - pnorm((k - ((1 - W) * (mu_A - theta_C_star) + delta)) / sqrt(V_samp))

avgPower <- function(mu_D, sigma_D2, delta, k)
  1 - pnorm((k - ((1 - W) * (mu_A - mu_D) + delta)) / sqrt(V_samp + (1 - W)^2 * sigma_D2))

calc_EUII <- function(power, t1e, N) {
  eps <- 1e-10
  p <- pmax(pmin(power, 1 - eps), eps); t <- pmax(pmin(t1e, 1 - eps), eps)

  log_dor <- qlogis(p) - qlogis(t)
  return(exp(log_dor / N))
} 

my_theme <- theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())
my_x_scale <- scale_x_continuous(trans = "log1p", breaks = c(0, 10, 100, 1000, 10000), labels = c("0\n(Cond.)", "10", "100", "1K", "10K"))

# --- T1E Data & Plots ---
df_t1e_loc <- data.frame(mu_D = seq(-90, -10, length.out = 100)) |>
  mutate(
    `Conditional T1E` = condPower(mu_D, 0, k_SC),
    `Average T1E` = avgPower(mu_D, sigma_A2, 0, k_SC)
  ) |>
  pivot_longer(cols = c(`Conditional T1E`, `Average T1E`), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Value")

sigmas <- c(0, 10^seq(0, 5, length.out = 99))
df_t1e_scale <- data.frame(sigma_D = sigmas) |>
  mutate(Avg_T1E = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 0, k_SC))

p1a <- ggplot(df_t1e_loc, aes(x = mu_D, y = Value, color = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed", color = "red", linewidth = 0.4) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average T1E" = col_avg, "Conditional T1E" = col_cond)) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * mu["D,C"] * ")"),
       y = "T1E") +
  my_theme
 
p1b <- ggplot(df_t1e_scale, aes(x = sigma_D, y = Avg_T1E)) +
  geom_line(color = "#E69F00", size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.5, linetype = "dashed", color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed", color = "red", linewidth = 0.4) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = sqrt(sigma_A2), linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  my_x_scale +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(0, 0.6)) +
  labs(x = expression(sigma["D,C"]),
       y = "avgT1E") +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p1a, p1b, ncol = 2)
@

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_power} shows the behavior of statistical power assuming $\mu_{D,C} = \mu_{A,C} = -50$. In the left panel $\sigma_{D,C} = \sigma_{A,C}$ and we vary $\delta$. As already observed, avgPower is always closer to $50\%$ than Conditional Power. The right panel shows how avgPower at $\delta = 60$ changes as design uncertainty $\sigma_{D,C}$ increases: when $\sigma_{D,C} = 0$ we obtain the conditional power, while as $\sigma_{D,C} \to \infty$, avgPower reaches $50\%$.

<<plot_power, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.height=4, fig.width=10, fig.cap="Left panel shows Conditional and Average Power evaluated across true treatment effects ($\\delta$), assuming aligned priors ($\\mu_{A,C} = \\mu_{D,C}$ and $\\sigma_{A,C} = \\sigma_{D,C}$). Right panel shows Average Power evaluated at $\\delta = 60$ as design uncertainty ($\\sigma_{D,C}$) increases, holding $\\mu_{D,C} = \\mu_{A,C}$. The vertical gray dotted lines indicate the points of prior alignment ($\\pi_D = \\pi_A$).">>=
# --- Power Data & Plots ---
df_pow_loc <- data.frame(delta = seq(0, 100, length.out = 100)) |>
  mutate(
    `Conditional Power` = condPower(mu_A, delta, k_SC),
    `Average Power` = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_SC)
  ) |>
  pivot_longer(cols = c(`Conditional Power`, `Average Power`), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Value")

df_pow_scale <- data.frame(sigma_D = sigmas) |>
  mutate(Avg_Power = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 60, k_SC))

p2a <- ggplot(df_pow_loc, aes(x = delta, y = Value, color = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average Power" = col_avg, "Conditional Power" = col_cond)) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"),
       y = "Power") +
  my_theme

p2b <- ggplot(df_pow_scale, aes(x = sigma_D, y = Avg_Power)) +
  geom_line(color = "#E69F00", size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.5, linetype = "dashed", color="black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = sqrt(sigma_A2), linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  my_x_scale +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(0.4, 1.0)) +
  labs(x = expression(sigma["D,C"]),
       y = "avgPower") +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p2a, p2b, ncol = 2)
@

\subsubsection{Sample Size Computation}
When performing sample size computation, it is reasonable to assume a correctly specified analysis prior ($\mu_{A,C} = \mu_{D,C}$ and $\sigma_{A,C} = \sigma_{D,C}$) and a common patient variance $\sigma^2$ across both arms, such that $v_C = \sigma^2/n_C$ and $v_T = \sigma^2/n_T$.
We can also define the ESS for the analysis prior $n_A$ ($n_A = n_D$ under the correctly specified prior assumption), and write $\sigma_{A,C}^2 = \sigma^2/n_A$.

Under these assumptions, the weight becomes $W = n_C/(n_C+n_A)$ and the bias term $(1-W)(\mu_{A,C}-\mu_{D,C})$ vanishes entirely. We further define two ratios:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $r_A = n_A/n_C$: the historical-to-control ESS ratio (implying $W = 1/(1+r_A)$).
    \item $r_T = n_T/n_C$: the allocation ratio.
\end{itemize}


Substituting the ESS parameterization into Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_closed_form} and simplifying yields:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avgpower_ess}
    \avgPow(\delta) = 1 - \Phi \left( z_p - \frac{\delta - q}{\sigma \sqrt{\dfrac{1}{n_C + n_A} + \dfrac{1}{n_T}}} \right).
\end{equation}
\Remark Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_ess} is identical to the Power of a two sample Z-test (Equation~\eqref{eq:ztest_power}) evaluated with $n_C^* = n_C + n_A$ effective control patients.

Setting Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_ess} equal to the target power $1-\beta$ and fixing the allocation ratio $r_T$ (so $n_T = r_T n_C$) yields:
$$ \frac{1}{n_C + n_A} + \frac{1}{r_T\, n_C} = \frac{(\delta - q)^2}{\sigma^2(z_p + z_{1-\beta})^2} =: \frac{1}{K} $$.
This results in a quadratic equation in terms of $n_C$:
$$ r_T\, n_C^2 + \bigl[r_T n_A - K(r_T + 1)\bigr]\, n_C - K n_A = 0 $$.
The positive root provides the closed-form solution for the required control sample size:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:n_c_formula}
    n_C = \frac{K(r_T+1) - r_T n_A + \sqrt{\bigl[K(r_T+1) - r_T n_A\bigr]^2 + 4\, r_T\, K\, n_A}}{2\, r_T}, \quad K = \frac{\sigma^2(z_p + z_{1 -\beta})^2}{(\delta - q)^2}.
\end{equation}
As $n_A \to 0$ (no historical borrowing), Equation~\eqref{eq:n_c_formula} converges to $n_C \to K(1 + 1/r_T)$, which is exactly the classical frequentist two-sample $Z$-test formula with unequal group sizes. 


Average power is therefore a more natural metric for sample size planning in this Bayesian framework. Under the alignment assumptions the two variance terms coincide ($V_\text{marg} = V_\delta$), 
which yields two practical advantages. First, the required sample size follows in closed form from Equation~\eqref{eq:n_c_formula}. Second, the historical sample size $n_A$ acquires a clean additive interpretation: it enters Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_ess} only through the sum $n_C + n_A$, so each historical pseudo-observation reduces the required number of control patients by exactly one.
Instead, solving $\text{Conditional Power} = 1-\beta$ for $n_C$ requires $z_p\sqrt{V_\delta} + z_{1-\beta}\sqrt{V_\text{samp}} = \delta - q$, where both $n_C$ and $n_A$ enter through $V_\delta$ and $V_\text{samp}$ in different ways. 
The sample size can then only be obtained by numerical inversion, and $n_A$ has no convenient interpretation.


\paragraph{Running example.} 
Using the parameters from the running example ($\sigma = 88$, $n_A = n_D = 20$, $r_T = 2$, $\delta = 60$, $q = 0$, $p = 0.95$), we obtain for a target power of 80\%:
$$ K = \frac{88^2 \times (1.645 + 0.842)^2}{60^2} \approx 13.30 $$.
Substituting into Equation~\eqref{eq:n_c_formula} gives $n_C \approx 11.5 \to 12$, so $n_C = 12$ control patients ($n_T = 24$, $n_{\text{trial}} = 36$) achieves just above $80\%$ average power. The actual design ($n_C = 20$) yields $\avgPow(60) \approx 92\%$, consistent with Figure~\ref{fig:plot_power} and with Equation~\eqref{eq:ztest_power} evaluated at $n_C^* = 40$.

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_avgpower_nc} shows how average power varies with $n_C$ for several values of $n_A$: borrowing more historical data shifts the power curve upwards, reducing the required $n_C$. 

<<plot_avgpower_nc, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=3.5, fig.width=6, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Average power varying control sample size $n_C$ at $\\delta = 60$ for varying $n_A$. The horizontal dashed line represents 80\\% power, while the two dotted verticals the minimum required $n_C$ (Eq.~\\ref{eq:n_c_formula} with $n_A=20$) and actual design value $n_C = 20$.">>=
avgPower_ess <- function(delta, n_C, n_A, r_T, sigma, z_p, q = 0) {
  n_T <- r_T * n_C
  1 - pnorm(z_p - (delta - q) / (sigma * sqrt(1 / (n_C + n_A) + 1 / n_T)))
}

n_C_seq <- seq(4, 40, by = 0.5)
n_A_vals <- c(0, 10, 20, 30)
df_ss <- do.call(rbind, lapply(n_A_vals, function(na) {
  data.frame(
    n_C   = n_C_seq,
    power = avgPower_ess(delta = 60, n_C = n_C_seq, n_A = na,
                         r_T = 2, sigma = 88, z_p = qnorm(0.95)),
    n_A   = factor(paste0("n_A = ", na))
  )
}))

#
K_req   <- 88^2 * (qnorm(0.95) + qnorm(0.80))^2 / 60^2 # 13.30
b_req   <- K_req * (2 + 1) - 2 * 20
n_C_req <- (b_req + sqrt(b_req^2 + 4 * 2 * K_req * 20)) / (2 * 2)  # 11.5

legend_labels <- expression(n[A] == 0, n[A] == 10, n[A] == 20, n[A] == 30)

ggplot(df_ss, aes(x = n_C, y = power, colour = n_A)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.8, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey50") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = n_C_req, linetype = "dotted", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 20,      linetype = "dotted", colour = "grey40") +
  annotate("text", x = n_C_req - 0.6, y = 0.15, label = paste0("n[C] == ", round(n_C_req, 1)),
           hjust = 1, size = 3, colour = "grey30", parse = TRUE) +
  annotate("text", x = 20 + 0.6, y = 0.15, label = "n[C] == 20",
           hjust = 0, size = 3, colour = "grey30", parse = TRUE) +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::percent_format(accuracy = 1), limits = c(0, 1)) +
  scale_colour_brewer(palette = "Set1", labels = legend_labels) +
  labs(x = expression(n[C]), y = "Average Power",
       colour = NULL, linetype = NULL) +
  my_theme +
  theme(legend.position = "right")
@


\subsubsection{Computational Framework \note{TO DO: decide whether to keep or not: it is just how the metric is computed in R}}
We will now define the computational framework for the average metric, obtained extending the \texttt{RBesT} package of \citet{weber_applying_2021}.

Let $y_T$ and $y_C$ denote the sample means for the treatment and control arms, respectively.

Success is determined by a decision function $D(y_T, y_C) \in \{0, 1\}$, which evaluates whether the posterior distribution of the treatment contrast, 
  $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$, satisfies a set of predefined conditions. For instance, a standard design requires the posterior probability to 
  exceed a specific threshold: $\Pr(\delta > q \mid y_T, y_C) > p$ for threshold $q$ and probability $p$.

When evaluating multiple conditions simultaneously, such as in Dual Criterion designs requiring both statistical significance and clinical relevance 
\citep{roychoudhury_beyond_2018}, the decision is formulated as the product of Heaviside step functions for each condition\footnote{Note that this product 
formulation naturally generalizes to an arbitrary number of conditions} $H(x)$, where $H(x) = 1$ for $x > 0$ and $0$ for $x \le 0$:

$$D(y_T, y_C) = \prod_{i} H\Big(\Pr(\delta \ge q_{c,i} \mid y_T, y_C) - p_{c,i}\Big)$$. 



The specification of priors, the sample sizes ($n_T$, $n_C$), and the decision function $D(y_T, y_C)$ uniquely define a conditional Decision Boundary 
$D_T(y_C)$ for each observed control outcome $y_C$,
$$D_T(y_C) = \max\{y_T : D(y_T, y_C) = 1\}$$. 
This represents the maximal threshold the treatment data $y_T$ must satisfy to declare success.

Given this boundary, we can compute the conditional probability of success $\Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T)$ required in Equation \ref{eq:m_joint}. Assuming
 true parameters $\theta_C$ and $\theta_T$, the overall probability of a success is the expectation of crossing the boundary $D_T(y_C)$ over the sampling
 distribution of the control data:
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:oc2S_general}
  \Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} F_T\left(D_T(y_C) \mid \theta_T\right) f_C\left(y_C \mid \theta_C\right) \, dy_C,
\end{equation}
where $F_T\left(\cdot \mid \theta_T\right)$ is the cumulative distribution function of the treatment statistic evaluated at the decision boundary, and
 $f_C\left(\cdot \mid \theta_C\right)$ is the probability density function of the control statistic.

Because we are working with normally distributed endpoints, the sampling distributions for the sample means are normal: 
$y_m \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_m, SEM_m^2)$ for $m \in \{T, C\}$, where $SEM_m = \sigma_m / \sqrt{n_m}$. Consequently, the general integral in 
Equation \ref{eq:oc2S_general} can be evaluated directly using the standard normal CDF ($\Phi$) and PDF ($\phi$):
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:oc2S_normal}
  \Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Phi\left(\frac{D_T(y_C) - \theta_T}{SEM_T}\right) \frac{1}{SEM_C} \phi\left(\frac{y_C - \theta_C}{SEM_C}\right) \, dy_C.
\end{equation}
 

\subsection{Dual Criterion}
\label{subsec:dual_criterion_methods}
Following the DC design of Section~\ref{subsec:dual_criterion_intro}, we extend the framework so that trial success requires two posterior conditions to hold 
simultaneously: a significance criterion against the null value $q$:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:dc_general}
    \Pr(\delta > q \mid \mathcal{D},\, \pi_A) \ge p,
\end{equation} 
and a clinical relevance criterion against a decision value $\DV > q$, requiring the posterior median to be clinically meaningful:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:dc_general_clin}
    \Pr(\delta > \DV \mid \mathcal{D},\, \pi_A) \ge 0.5.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Analytical Results under Normal Priors for the Dual Criterion}
Under the assumption of Normality for both analysis and design priors, the Dual Criterion (DC) reduces to a single condition on the posterior mean $\hat{\delta}$: the significance criterion~\eqref{eq:dc_general} is, 
as in Equation~\eqref{eq:threshold_k}, equivalent to $\hat{\delta} > q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}}$, while the clinical criterion~\eqref{eq:dc_general_clin} is equivalent 
to $\hat{\delta} \ge \DV$, since the Normal posterior median equals its mean. Combining the two,
\begin{equation} \label{eq:k_dc}
    \hat{\delta} > k_{\DC}, \qquad k_{\DC} = \max\!\left(\DV, \; q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}}\right) = \max(\DV, k).
\end{equation}
with $V_\delta = \sigma_{A,C}^2 v_C / (\sigma_{A,C}^2 + v_C) + v_T$. 

The decision rule is thus the only change relative to the Single Criterion (SC) design (the standard design that applies the significance criterion~\eqref{eq:dc_general} alone): the estimator $\hat{\delta}$, its sampling distribution, and
its marginal distribution are unchanged. Replacing the threshold $k$ by $k_{\DC}$ in Equations~\eqref{eq:power_closed_form}-\eqref{eq:avgt1e_closed_form} 
gives all four metrics directly. For example, the average Power and T1E become
\begin{align}
    \avgPow_{\DC}(\delta) &= 1 - \Phi\!\left( \frac{k_{\DC} - \big[(1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \mu_{D,C}) + \delta\big]}{\sqrt{V_{\text{marg}}}} \right), \label{eq:dc_avgpower} \\[4pt]
    \avgTIE_{\DC} &= 1 - \Phi\!\left( \frac{k_{\DC} - (1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \mu_{D,C})}{\sqrt{V_{\text{marg}}}} \right). \label{eq:dc_avgt1e}
\end{align}
The conditional metrics follow by the same substitution. 
   
\Remark Since $k_{\DC} \ge k$, the DC is more conservative: $\Pow_{\DC} \le \Pow_{\SC}$ 
and $\avgPow_{\DC} \le \avgPow_{\SC}$ at fixed sample size. 
Moreover, when analysis and design priors coincide and $q = 0$, the average T1E equals the nominal $\alpha$ when $z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} \ge \DV$, and drops below $\alpha$ 
when $z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} < \DV$. The exact control of the SC is therefore preserved or tightened, never violated.

\subsubsection{Sample Size Computation}
The sample size of a DC design must be at least $n_{\min}$, where $n_{\min}$ denotes the minimum sample size such that a point estimate at the decision value, $\hat{\delta} = \DV$, is statistically significant \citep{roychoudhury_beyond_2018}.
To compute $n_{\min}$, we equate the two thresholds in Equation~\eqref{eq:k_dc}, $k = \DV$, under the same assumptions used for the power calculation of Equation~\eqref{eq:n_c_formula}: a correctly specified analysis prior, a
common known variance $\sigma^2$ in both arms ($v_C = \sigma^2/n_C$, $v_T = \sigma^2/n_T$) and an analysis prior summarised by its effective sample size
$n_A$.

We finally obtain: 
$$ \frac{1}{n_C + n_A} + \frac{1}{r_T\, n_C} = \frac{(\DV - q)^2}{\sigma^2 z_p^2} =: \frac{1}{M}, $$
which is the same equation solved for the sample size computations in the SC case, with the power constant $K = \sigma^2(z_p + z_{1-\beta})^2/(\delta-q)^2$ replaced by $M = \sigma^2 z_p^2/(\DV-q)^2$, that is, evaluated at $z_{1-\beta} = 0$ and $\delta = \DV$. As in that derivation, this gives a quadratic in $n_C$:
$$ r_T\, n_C^2 + \bigl[r_T n_A - M(r_T+1)\bigr]\, n_C - M n_A = 0, $$
whose positive root is the minimum control arm size:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:dc_nmin_general}
    n_{C,\min} = \frac{M(r_T + 1) - r_T n_A + \sqrt{\big[M(r_T + 1) - r_T n_A\big]^2 + 4\, r_T\, M\, n_A}}{2\, r_T},
\end{equation}
identical to Equation~\eqref{eq:n_c_formula} under the replacement $K \mapsto M$. Setting $z_{1-\beta} = 0$ means targeting a $50\%$ power, so requiring the two thresholds to coincide is the same as requiring $50\%$ power at $\delta = \DV$. This result, well known for the conditional power \citep{roychoudhury_beyond_2018}, thus extends also to the average power under aligned priors.
In the case of no borrowing ($n_A \to 0$) with a balanced design ($r_T = 1$), Equation~\eqref{eq:dc_nmin_general} reduces to $n_{C,\min} = 2M$ control patients and, by balance, $n_{T,\min} = 2M$ treatment patients. The total minimum sample size is therefore:
$$ n_{\min} = n_{C,\min} + n_{T,\min} = 2M + 2M = \frac{4\, z_p^2\, \sigma^2}{(\DV-q)^2}, $$
which recovers the DC formula of \citet{roychoudhury_beyond_2018} for a Normal endpoint, where the treatment in a balanced trial has variance $4\sigma^2/n_{\min}$.

 
\paragraph{Running example.} 
We extend the running example with a clinical relevance criterion. In addition to the significance requirement $\Pr(\delta > 0 \mid \mathcal{D},\, \pi_A) \ge 0.95$, success now also requires $\Pr(\delta > 50 \mid \mathcal{D},\, \pi_A) \ge 0.5$, that is, a decision value $\DV = 50$. With $\sigma = 88$ and $z_p = \Phi^{-1}(0.95) = 1.645$, we obtain:
$$
    M = \frac{\sigma^2 z_p^2}{(\DV - q)^2} = \frac{88^2 \times 1.645^2}{50^2} \approx 8.38.
$$
Using the running example allocation $r_T = 2$ and historical $\mathrm{ESS}$ $n_A = 20$, the minimum sample size follows from Equation~\eqref{eq:dc_nmin_general}, giving $n_{C,\min} \approx 6.2$, so $n_{C,\min} = 7$ control patients ($n_{T,\min} = 14$ and $n_{\min}=21$). Without borrowing ($n_A \to 0$) the same requirement needs $n_{C,\min} = M(1 + 1/r_T) \approx 12.6$ control patients. 
The actual design ($n_C = 20$) is well above this minimum: $k = q + z_p\sqrt{V_\delta} \approx 32 < \DV = 50$, and the average T1E falls below nominal: $\avgTIE = 1 - \Phi(\DV/\sqrt{V_\delta}) = 1 - \Phi(50/19.7) \approx 0.6\%$ rather than $5\%$. 

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_t1e_ratio} shows, on the left, the ratio of the T1E of the DC design to that of the SC design (a value below $1$ means the relevance threshold lowers the metric) and, on the right, the absolute avgT1E of both designs. The conditional ratio is shrunk more than the average one, and the 
avgT1E of the DC design is controlled more uniformly: on the right panel it stays below the nominal $\alpha = 0.05$ over a wider range of true control means than the SC design.

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_power_ratio} shows, on the left, the conditional and average power of the DC design: as for the SC design, the average power lies closer to $50\%$ than the conditional one. On the right it shows the average power of both designs. The DC design has uniformly lower avgPower and, as expected, its power at the decision value sits exactly at $50\%$.

<<plot_t1e_ratio, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Left panel shows the ratio of the DC to the SC T1E under Location Shift (varying $\\mu_{D,C}$, assuming $\\sigma_{D,C} = \\sigma_{A,C}$), for the Conditional ratio (evaluated at $\\theta_C^* = \\mu_{D,C}$) and the Average ratio. Right panel shows the corresponding average T1E of the two designs. The vertical gray dotted line indicates the prior mean $\\mu_{A,C} = -50$, and the red dashed line the nominal value of $0.05$.">>=
# Dual-criterion threshold for the running example (k_SC defined in setup) 
DV   <- 50  
k_DC <- max(DV, k_SC)  

df_rt1e_loc <- data.frame(mu_D = seq(-90, -10, length.out = 100)) |>
  mutate(
    Conditional = condPower(mu_D, 0, k_DC) / condPower(mu_D, 0, k_SC),
    Average     = avgPower(mu_D, sigma_A2, 0, k_DC) / avgPower(mu_D,  sigma_A2, 0, k_SC)
  ) |>
  pivot_longer(cols = c(Conditional, Average), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Ratio")

df_t1e_abs <- data.frame(mu_D = seq(-90, -10, length.out = 100)) |>
  mutate(  
     DC         = avgPower(mu_D, sigma_A2, 0, k_DC),
    SC = avgPower(mu_D, sigma_A2, 0, k_SC) 
  ) |>
  pivot_longer(cols = c(DC, SC), names_to = "Criterion", values_to = "avgT1E")

pr1a <- ggplot(df_rt1e_loc, aes(mu_D, Ratio, color = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed", color = "black", linewidth = 0.5) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average" = col_avg, "Conditional" = col_cond)) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * mu["D,C"] * ")"),
       y = "T1E ratio (DC / SC)") + my_theme

pr1b <- ggplot(df_t1e_abs, aes(mu_D, avgT1E, color = Criterion)) +
  geom_line(size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed", color = "red", linewidth = 0.4) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("DC" = col_dc, "SC" = col_sc)) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * mu["D,C"] * ")"),
       y = "Average T1E") + my_theme

grid.arrange(pr1a, pr1b, ncol = 2)
@

<<plot_power_ratio, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Left panel shows the Conditional and Average Power of the DC design evaluated across true treatment effects ($\\delta$), assuming aligned priors ($\\mu_{A,C} = \\mu_{D,C}$ and $\\sigma_{A,C} = \\sigma_{D,C}$). Right panel shows the Average Power of the DC and SC designs across the same effects. The vertical gray dotted line marks the decision value $\\DV = 50$.">>=
df_pow_dc <- data.frame(delta = seq(0, 100, length.out = 100)) |>
  mutate(
    `Conditional Power` = condPower(mu_A, delta, k_DC),
    `Average Power`     = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_DC)
  ) |>
  pivot_longer(cols = c(`Conditional Power`, `Average Power`),
               names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Value")

df_pow_abs <- data.frame(delta = seq(0, 100, length.out = 100)) |>
  mutate(
    DC         = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_DC),
    SC = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_SC)
  ) |>
  pivot_longer(cols = c(DC, SC), names_to = "Criterion", values_to = "avgPower")

pr2a <- ggplot(df_pow_dc, aes(delta, Value, color = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = DV, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average Power" = col_avg, "Conditional Power" = col_cond)) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"), y = "Power (DC)") + my_theme

pr2b <- ggplot(df_pow_abs, aes(delta, avgPower, color = Criterion)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = DV, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("DC" = col_dc, "SC" = col_sc)) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"), y = "Average Power") + my_theme

grid.arrange(pr2a, pr2b, ncol = 2)
@


\subsection{Group Sequential Designs}
\label{subsec:sequential}

A group sequential design (GSD) extends the fixed design by allowing the trial to stop early at one or more prespecified interim looks, either for efficacy or futility. 

\subsubsection{Design Setup}
\label{subsubsec:seq_setup}

Let $K$ denote the total number of stages, with $K - 1$ interim analyses followed by a final analysis. Patients are recruited in $K$ cohorts. The cumulative sample sizes for arm $m \in \{T, C\}$ at stage $k \in \{1, \dots, K\}$ are $n_{m,1} < n_{m,2} < \dots < n_{m,K}$, with $\Delta n_{m,k} = n_{m,k} - n_{m,k-1}$ additional patients enrolled between stages (setting $n_{m,0} = 0$). The total planned sample size is $n_{\text{trial}} = n_{T,K} + n_{C,K}$, reached only if the trial runs to the final analysis.

Under normal sampling with known variances, the cumulative sample means are distributed as:
\begin{equation*}
  \bar{y}_{m,k} \mid \theta_m \sim \mathcal{N}\!\left(\theta_m,\; \frac{\sigma_m^2}{n_{m,k}}\right).
\end{equation*}
The analysis prior $\pi_A(\theta_C)$ and the vague treatment prior are updated at each stage using the cumulative arm means, yielding the stage $k$ posterior for the treatment contrast $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$:
\begin{equation*}
  \delta \mid \bar{y}_{C,k}, \bar{y}_{T,k} \sim \mathcal{N}(\hat{\delta}_k, V_{\delta,k}),
\end{equation*}
where $\hat{\delta}_k$ and $V_{\delta,k}$ follow from the Normal-Normal conjugacy derivations in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_normal_priors}.

\paragraph{Stopping Rules at the Interim}
There are two types of stopping rules: efficacy and futility.
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Efficacy:} the trial stops early for success if
  \begin{equation*}
    \Pr(\delta > q_{k, \text{eff}} \mid \bar{y}_{C,k}, \bar{y}_{T,k}) > p_{k, \text{eff}}.
  \end{equation*}
  \item \textbf{Futility:} the trial stops early without success if
  \begin{equation*}
    \Pr(\delta < q_{k, \text{fut}} \mid \bar{y}_{C,k}, \bar{y}_{T,k}) > p_{k, \text{fut}}.
  \end{equation*}
\end{itemize}
For each type of stopping rule, any number of criteria can be combined: for example,  
the Dual Criterion of Section~\ref{subsec:dual_criterion_methods} can be used for efficacy. 

If neither rule triggers, the trial continues to the next stage. Every trial
therefore terminates in exactly one of the following mutually exclusive outcomes:
an efficacy stop at some stage $k$, a futility stop at some interim stage $k < K$, 
or a failure at the final analysis. The thresholds may in general be stage specific, 
and futility rules may be restricted to a subset of the interim looks.

\subsubsection{Operating Characteristics}
\label{subsubsec:seq_oc}


To obtain conditional and average operating characteristics, we follow the traditional method
of simulating the trial repeatedly using a Monte Carlo approach \citep{berry2010bayesian}.

\paragraph{A Single Simulated Replicate}
Fixing the true parameters $(\theta_C^*, \delta)$, a replicate proceeds stage by stage. At stage $k$, new cohort means 
are drawn independently for both arms:
\begin{equation*}
  \bar{y}_{m,\Delta k} \mid \theta_m \sim \mathcal{N}\!\left(\theta_m, \frac{\sigma_m^2}{\Delta n_{m,k}}\right), \qquad m \in \{T, C\},
\end{equation*}
these are then combined with the previous cumulative means:
\begin{equation*}
  \bar{y}_{m,k} = \frac{n_{m,k-1}\,\bar{y}_{m,k-1} + \Delta n_{m,k}\,\bar{y}_{m,\Delta k}}{n_{m,k}}.
\end{equation*}
The efficacy and futility criteria of Section~\ref{subsubsec:seq_setup} are then evaluated on the simulated $(\bar{y}_{T,k}, \bar{y}_{C,k})$. 
The replicate stops for efficacy or futility the first time either criterion is met, or continues to the next stage. If it reaches stage $K$
without either, it is recorded as a failure.

\paragraph{Conditional Operating Characteristics}
Repeating this over many independent trials, all sharing the same $(\theta_C^*,\delta)$, gives the conditional stopping probabilities, estimated
as the proportion of replicates stopping for efficacy (or futility) at each stage:
\begin{align*}
  P_{\text{eff},k}(\theta_C^*, \delta)  &= \Pr(\text{efficacy stop at stage } k \mid \theta_C^*, \delta), \\
  P_{\text{fut},k}(\theta_C^*, \delta)  &= \Pr(\text{futility stop at stage } k \mid \theta_C^*, \delta),
\end{align*}
where a non success at the final analysis is counted as a futility stop at stage $K$, so that $\sum_{k=1}^{K} \big(P_{\text{eff},k} + P_{\text{fut},k}\big) = 1$. 
The overall conditional Power and T1E follow directly:
\begin{align*}
  \Pow(\theta_C^*, \delta) &= \sum_{k=1}^{K} P_{\text{eff},k}(\theta_C^*, \delta), \\
  \TIE(\theta_C^*)            &= \Pow(\theta_C^*, \delta = 0).
\end{align*}
 

Averaging each interim's sample size, we obtain the conditional expected sample size:
\begin{equation*}
  \E[N \mid \theta_C^*, \delta] = \sum_{k=1}^{K} n_k \big(P_{\text{eff},k} + P_{\text{fut},k}\big),
\end{equation*}
where $n_k = n_{T,k} + n_{C,k}$ is the cumulative total sample size at stage $k$.

\paragraph{Average Operating Characteristics}
The average operating characteristics require only one change to the trial of the previous paragraphs: 
rather than fixing $\theta_C$ at $\theta_C^*$ for every replicate, a value is drawn from the design prior 
$\pi_D(\theta_C)$ independently for each trial, with $\theta_T$ set to keep $\delta$ at the value of interest,
 before that replicate is simulated exactly as above. 
The conditional operating characteristics of the previous paragraph are recovered exactly as the special case where 
every replicate draws the same fixed $\theta_C^*$.



\subsection{The Experimental Unit Information Index}
\label{subsec:euii}

The EUII \citep{held2025balancing}, is a metric that normalizes the diagnostic odds ratio (DOR) with the sample size. 

\subsubsection{The Diagnostic Odds Ratio}
The DOR is a summary of a binary test's discriminatory performance borrowed 
from the diagnostic accuracy literature \citep{glas2003diagnostic}. For a 
statistical hypothesis test with T1E rate $\alpha$ and 
power $1 - \beta$, the DOR is defined as the ratio of the odds 
of rejection under the alternative to the odds of rejection under 
the null:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:classical_dor}
    \DOR
    = \frac{(1-\beta)/\beta}{\alpha/(1-\alpha)}
    = \frac{\Pow/(1-\Pow)}
           {\TIE/(1-\TIE)}
    = \frac{\text{LR}^+}{\text{LR}^-},
\end{equation}
%
where $\text{LR}^+ = \Pow/\TIE$ and $\text{LR}^- = (1-\Pow)/(1-\TIE)$ are the likelihood ratios of a positive and a negative outcome respectively.
A $\DOR > 1$ indicates that the odds of a positive
decision are higher under the alternative than under the null,
which holds for any test with power exceeding its T1E
rate. The DOR increases with power and decreases with T1E inflation.

\Remark The DOR also has a Bayesian interpretation: it equals the ratio of the posterior odds of the alternative 
given a positive result to the posterior odds given a negative one:
\begin{equation*}
  \DOR = \frac{\Pr(H_1\mid\text{success})/\Pr(H_0\mid\text{success})}{\Pr(H_1\mid\text{no success})/\Pr(H_0\mid\text{no success})}.
\end{equation*}
The DOR, and hence the EUII, is therefore simultaneously frequentist, built from Power and T1E alone, and Bayesian, quantifying how much 
more the alternative is favoured after a positive result than a negative one.


\subsubsection{EUII for fixed designs}
The classical EUII of \cite{held2025balancing} normalizes the DOR
by the sample size $n_{\text{trial}} = n_C + n_T$ to obtain a per-unit measure of
evidentiary efficiency:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:classical_euii}
    \EUII = \DOR^{1/n_{\text{trial}}}.
\end{equation}
This is the geometric mean contribution of a single experimental unit to the design's total discriminatory evidence, so
that a value $\EUII > 1$ means each additional unit increases, on average, the odds of correctly distinguishing the
alternative from the null.

We can define the Average EUII ($\EUII_{avg}$) from avgPower and avgT1E. The Average DOR is:
$$\DOR_{avg}(\delta) = \frac{\avgPow(\delta) / (1 - \avgPow(\delta))}{\avgTIE / (1 - \avgTIE)},$$
and normalizing by the trial sample size $n_{\text{trial}}$ yields
\begin{equation} \label{eq:euii_avg}
    \EUII_{avg}(\delta) = \left[ \DOR_{avg}(\delta) \right]^{\frac{1}{n_{\text{trial}}}}.
\end{equation}

For the remainder of the manuscript, we refer to $\EUII_{avg}$ simply as EUII for ease of notation. In fact, consistent with the underlying metrics, 
the Conditional EUII represents the specific case where $\pi_D(\theta_C)$ degenerates to a Dirac point mass at $\theta_C^*$.

\Remark For a non-degenerate design prior, the average EUII is not the design prior expectation of the pointwise DOR:
$$\DOR_{avg}(\delta) \neq \int \DOR_{cond}(\theta_C, \delta) \pi_D(\theta_C) d\theta_C,$$
because the ratio of expectations does not, in general, equal the expectation of a ratio. 


\subsubsection{Which Sample Size to Use}

The definition in Equation~\eqref{eq:classical_euii} leaves open a question that is crucial under Bayesian borrowing: what is $n$?

The robust MAP analysis prior blends an informative MAP component with a vague component, controlled by a robustness weight $\omega \in [0, 1]$: at $\omega = 0$ the prior reduces to the full MAP prior and at $\omega = 1$ it becomes entirely vague. The effective sample size $n_{\text{A}}$ of the analysis prior therefore depends on $\omega$: it is largest for the full MAP ($\omega = 0$) and shrinks to zero for the vague prior ($\omega = 1$). One might therefore argue for a penalized denominator $n_{\text{eff}} = n_{\text{trial}} + n_{\text{A}}$, attributing part of the evidentiary gain to the historical patients.

We argue that this is the wrong normalisation. The EUII is a design tool: it answers how much evidence each newly enrolled patient contributes. Historical patients are already given. They have been collected and encoded in the prior before the current trial begins. Including $n_{\text{A}}$ in the denominator conflates two separate decisions: the prior choice (which $\omega$ to use) and the trial design (how many new patients to enroll). This conflation is particularly misleading because a more informative prior (low $\omega$, high $n_{\text{A}}$) would be penalised in the denominator for the very information it provides, while a vague prior ($\omega = 1$, $n_{\text{A}} = 0$) would not. The denominator should reflect the quantity the investigator actually controls.

We therefore use $n = n_{\text{trial}}$ throughout, in Equation~\eqref{eq:euii_avg} and in the conditional and asymptotic forms below: the
number of patients enrolled in the current trial, the decision variable of the trial design.

\paragraph{Running example.}
In Figure~\ref{fig:plot_euii}, EUII is plotted for the running example. The left panel shows that Average EUII provides a more conservative value than Conditional EUII.
The right panel shows the Average EUII as a function of $\sigma_{D,C}$.


<<plot_euii, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.height=4, fig.width=10, fig.cap="EUII for the running example. Left: Conditional and Average EUII across $\\delta$ under aligned priors. Right: Average EUII at $\\delta = 60$ versus design uncertainty $\\sigma_{D,C}$; the dotted line marks alignment ($\\pi_D = \\pi_A$).">>=
# --- EUII Data & Plots ---
df_euii_loc <- data.frame(delta = seq(0, 100, length.out = 100)) |>
  mutate(
    Cond_EUII = calc_EUII(condPower(mu_A, delta, k_SC), condPower(mu_A, 0, k_SC), N_total),
    Avg_EUII  = calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_SC), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, 0, k_SC), N_total)
  ) |> pivot_longer(cols = c(Cond_EUII, Avg_EUII), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Value")

df_euii_scale <- data.frame(sigma_D = sigmas) |>
  mutate(Avg_EUII = calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 60, k_SC), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 0, k_SC), N_total))

p3a <- ggplot(df_euii_loc, aes(x = delta, y = Value, color = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Avg_EUII" = col_avg, "Cond_EUII" = col_cond),
                     labels = c("Avg_EUII"  = expression(EUII[avg]),
                                "Cond_EUII" = expression(EUII[cond]))) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"), y = "EUII") + my_theme

p3b <- ggplot(df_euii_scale, aes(x = sigma_D, y = Avg_EUII)) +
  geom_line(color = col_avg, size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1.0, linetype = "dashed", color="black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = sqrt(sigma_A2), linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  my_x_scale +
  labs(x = expression(sigma["D,C"]), y = expression(EUII[avg])) + my_theme

grid.arrange(p3a, p3b, ncol = 2)
@

\paragraph{Dual Criterion}
Figure~\ref{fig:plot_euii_ratio} plots the EUII of the DC and the SC. It is restricted to $\delta > \DV = 50$, since inspecting values below that threshold would not be meaningful for the DC (Power $< 50\%$).
Noticeably, the two EUII curves cross: for effects moderately above DV the DC design has the higher EUII, but as $\delta$ grows the ratio declines, and for large effects the DC design has the lower EUII.
This happens because raising the success threshold has two opposing effects on the EUII. First, it lowers the avgT1E by a fixed amount and, since the avgT1E is computed under $\delta = 0$, this gain is the same whatever the true effect.
Second, it lowers the avgPower, and this loss grows with $\delta$: the SC design reaches $\avgPow \approx 1$ sooner, leading to a steep increase in the avgPower odds.
For moderate effects the fixed reduction in avgT1E outweighs the small loss of avgPower, so the DC design has higher EUII, while for large effects the avgPower loss dominates. \note{I also tried to evaluate $\EUII^{\DC} = \EUII^{\SC}$ as a function of $\delta$. This could lead to a closed form intersection value for $\delta$, but I can't solve it algebraically. TO DO}

<<plot_euii_ratio, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Left panel shows the ratio of the DC to the SC EUII, for the Conditional and Average metric, evaluated across true treatment effects (aligned priors $\\mu_{A,C} = \\mu_{D,C}$ and $\\sigma_{A,C} = \\sigma_{D,C}$). Right panel shows the corresponding EUII of the two designs.">>=
df_reuii_loc <- data.frame(delta = seq(50, 100, length.out = 100)) |>
  mutate(
    Conditional = calc_EUII(condPower(mu_A, delta, k_DC), condPower(mu_A, 0, k_DC), N_total) /
                  calc_EUII(condPower(mu_A, delta, k_SC), condPower(mu_A, 0, k_SC), N_total),
    Average     = calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_DC), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, 0, k_DC), N_total) /
                  calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_SC), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, 0, k_SC), N_total)
  ) |>
  pivot_longer(cols = c(Conditional, Average), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Ratio")

df_euii_abs <- data.frame(delta = seq(50, 100, length.out = 100)) |>
  mutate(
    DC         = calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_DC), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, 0, k_DC), N_total),
    SC = calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta, k_SC), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, 0, k_SC), N_total)
  ) |>
  pivot_longer(cols = c(DC, SC), names_to = "Criterion", values_to = "avgEUII")

pr3a <- ggplot(df_reuii_loc, aes(delta, Ratio, color = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed", color = "black", linewidth = 0.5) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average" = col_avg, "Conditional" = col_cond)) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"), y = "EUII ratio (DC / SC)") + my_theme

pr3b <- ggplot(df_euii_abs, aes(delta, avgEUII, color = Criterion)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed", color = "black", linewidth = 0.5) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("DC" = col_dc, "SC" = col_sc)) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"), y = "EUII") + my_theme

grid.arrange(pr3a, pr3b, ncol = 2)
@

\subsubsection{Asymptotic Value}
Under the single Normal model, the EUII converges to a finite value:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:euii_asymptotic}
  \lim_{n_C \to \infty} \EUII(\delta)
  = \exp\!\left[\frac{r_T\big[(\delta - q)^2 + q^2\big]}{2\sigma^2 (1 + r_T)^2}\right].
\end{equation}
Explicit derivations are provided in Appendix~\ref{sec:app_asymptotic}. 

\Remark This value is identical to the two-sample result of \citet{held2025balancing} by setting $r_T = 1$, $\sigma = 1$ and $q = 0$.
Moreover, the allocation ratio enters only through $r_T/(1 + r_T)^2$, so Equation~\ref{eq:euii_asymptotic} is maximised by a balanced design ($r_T = 1$).

Importantly, the asymptotic value is independent of the analysis and design priors, therefore it is reasonable to expect the MAP mixture
and its robustified versions to reach the same asymptotic value. However, we cannot confirm this numerically, because evaluating
a mixture of Normals relies on numerical intergration that becomes instable as $n_C \to \infty$.

\paragraph{Dual Criterion}
Under the single Normal model, we can derive the asymptotic EUII of the Dual Criterion (see Appendix~\ref{sec:app_asymptotic} for details):
\begin{equation}\label{eq:euii_asymptotic_dc}
  \lim_{n_C \to \infty} \EUII^{\DC}(\delta)
  = \exp\!\left[\frac{r_T\big[(\delta - \DV)^2 + \DV^2\big]}{2\sigma^2 (1 + r_T)^2}\right].
\end{equation}
\Remark This is the SC asymptotic value (Equation~\eqref{eq:euii_asymptotic}) with $q$ replaced by DV. This means that asymptotically the DC behaves like a superiority test against the clinical margin DV.
Because $(\delta - \DV)^2 + \DV^2 < \delta^2$ whenever $0 < \DV < \delta$, the DC asymptotic value is strictly below the SC one. This can be seen in the left panel of Figure~\ref{fig:plot_euii_asymptotic},
where the asymptotic values are plotted for the running example. It is also possible to notice, in the right panel, that the DC has larger EUII for smaller sample sizes and the SC overcomes it after a certain value. 


<<plot_euii_asymptotic, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.height=4, fig.width=12, fig.cap="SC versus DC EUII for the running example. Left: asymptotic ($n_C \\to \\infty$) values as a function of $\\delta$. The DC asymptotic value is shown only for $\\delta > \\DV$. Right: EUII at $\\delta = 60$ as a function of the control size $n_C$ (log scale), with the corresponding asymptotic values as dashed lines.">>=
library(gridExtra)
sigma <- 88; rT_a <- 2; DV_a <- 50; n_A_a <- 20; z_p_a <- qnorm(0.95)
cols_euii <- c("DC" = col_dc, "SC" = col_sc)

## asymptotic value with limiting threshold kappa (q for SC, DV for DC)
euii_asymptotic <- function(delta, kappa)
  exp(rT_a * ((delta - kappa)^2 + kappa^2) / (2 * sigma^2 * (1 + rT_a)^2))

## --- Left panel: asymptotic values as a function of delta ---
dd <- seq(0, 100, length.out = 200)
df_asym <- rbind(
  data.frame(delta = dd,             Criterion = "SC", EUII = euii_asymptotic(dd, 0)),
  data.frame(delta = dd[dd >= DV_a], Criterion = "DC", EUII = euii_asymptotic(dd[dd >= DV_a], DV_a))
)
p_left <- ggplot(df_asym, aes(delta, EUII, color = Criterion)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.1) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = DV_a, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = cols_euii) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"),
       y = "Asymptotic EUII" ) + my_theme

## --- Right panel: finite-n EUII vs n_C at delta = 60, with asymptotes ---
Lstable <- function(x) pnorm(x, log.p = TRUE) - pnorm(x, lower.tail = FALSE, log.p = TRUE)  # logit Phi in log space

euii_finite <- function(n_C, criterion, delta = 60) {
  s <- sigma * sqrt(1 / (n_C + n_A_a) + 1 / (rT_a * n_C))   # see Formula (12). avgPower for aligned priors

  k  <- z_p_a * s                                          # SC threshold (q = 0)
  if (criterion == "DC") k <- max(k, DV_a)                 # DC threshold = max(k_SC, DV)
  exp((Lstable((delta - k) / s) - Lstable(-k / s)) / (n_C * (1 + rT_a)))
}

nc <- exp(seq(log(20), log(1e5), length.out = 200))

df_nc <- rbind(
  data.frame(n_C = nc, Criterion = "SC", EUII = sapply(nc, euii_finite, criterion = "SC")),
  data.frame(n_C = nc, Criterion = "DC", EUII = sapply(nc, euii_finite, criterion = "DC"))
)

asy <- data.frame(Criterion = c("SC", "DC"),
                  asy_value = c(euii_asymptotic(60, 0), euii_asymptotic(60, DV_a)))

p_right <- ggplot(df_nc, aes(n_C, EUII, color = Criterion)) +
  geom_hline(data = asy, aes(yintercept = asy_value, color = Criterion),
             linetype = "dashed", linewidth = 0.5, inherit.aes = FALSE) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.1) +
  scale_x_log10() +
  scale_color_manual(values = cols_euii) +
  labs(x = expression("Control sample size " * n[C] * " (log scale)"),
       y = expression(EUII * " (" * delta * " = 60)")) + my_theme

grid.arrange(p_left, p_right, ncol = 2)

