## (Analysis prior == Design prior ). Maybe it is numerical stabiity ?
@


\subsubsection{EUII for Sequential Designs}
\label{subsubsec:seq_euii}

In a sequential design, the sample size is a random variable, so we denote it by $N$ to distinguish it from a fixed sample size $n$. Following \citet{held2025balancing}, we can write $N^+$ and $N^-$ as conditional random variables:
$N^+ = N \mid \text{success}$ and $N^- = N \mid \text{no success}$\footnote{We are assuming that the trial design is nonsingular, i.e. both $\Pr(\{S = \text{success}\})$ and $\Pr(\{S = \text{no success}\})$ are larger than zero.}.

Thus, the EUII becomes:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:seq_euii}
  \EUII = \frac{(\text{LR}^+)^{\E[1/N^+]}}{(\text{LR}^-)^{\E[1/N^-]}}.
\end{equation}



The exponents $\E[1/N^+]$ and $\E[1/N^-]$ are expected inverse sample sizes. Writing $N_0^+ = N^+ \mid H_0$, $N_1^+ = N^+ \mid H_1$ 
for the sample size of successful trials under each, likewise $N_0^- = N^- \mid H_0$, $N_1^- = N^- \mid H_1$ for unsuccessful trials, we have:
\begin{align*}
  \E[1/N^+] &= \E[1/N_0^+]\cdot\Pr(H_0 \mid \text{success}) + \E[1/N_1^+]\cdot\Pr(H_1 \mid \text{success}) \\
  \E[1/N^-] &= \E[1/N_0^-]\cdot\Pr(H_0 \mid \text{no success}) + \E[1/N_1^-]\cdot\Pr(H_1 \mid \text{no success}).
\end{align*}

Since $N_i^+$ can only take the values $n_1,\dots,n_K$, we can write:
\begin{align}
  \E[1/N_i^+] &= \sum_{k=1}^{K} \frac{1}{n_k}\,\Pr(\text{stop at } k \mid \text{success}, H_i), \label{eq:seq_inv_enp} \\[6pt]
  \E[1/N_i^-] &= \sum_{k=1}^{K} \frac{1}{n_k}\,\Pr(\text{stop at } k \mid \text{no success}, H_i). \label{eq:seq_inv_enm}
\end{align} 
Note that $\E[1/N^+\mid\theta_C, \delta_i]\neq 1/\E[N^+\mid\theta_C, \delta_i]$ in general (Jensen's inequality).

The posterior probabilities $\Pr(H_0 \mid \cdot)$ and $\Pr(H_1 \mid \cdot)$ in the expected inverse sample size formulas above follow from the prior probability $\Pr(H_1)$ through Bayes' theorem in odds form: the prior odds
$$\Odds(H_1) = \frac{\Pr(H_1)}{1-\Pr(H_1)},$$
are multiplied by the likelihood ratio of the observed outcome, $\text{LR}^+$ for a success and $\text{LR}^-$ otherwise, to give the posterior odds
$$\Odds(H_1\mid\text{success}) = \text{LR}^+\cdot\Odds(H_1), \qquad \Odds(H_1\mid\text{no success}) = \text{LR}^-\cdot\Odds(H_1),$$
which are converted back to probabilities, $\Pr(H_1\mid\cdot) = \Odds(H_1\mid\cdot)/(1+\Odds(H_1\mid\cdot))$ and $\Pr(H_0\mid\cdot) = 1-\Pr(H_1\mid\cdot)$. 
Specifying $\Pr(H_1)$ depends on the scientific context: in clinical research a number of 10\% for the percentage of effective treatments in
Phase II or III trials has been suggested \citep{staquet1979delta, simon1982randomized}. In discovery-oriented research this probability is expected to be even lower \citep{ioannidis2005most}.

 
When the design never stops early, $\E[N^+ \mid \theta_C, \delta] = \E[N^- \mid \theta_C, \delta] = n_{\text{trial}}$ for all $\theta_C$, so $\E[1/N^+] = \E[1/N^-] = 1/n_{\text{trial}}$ and Equation~\eqref{eq:seq_euii} reduces exactly to the fixed design formula~\eqref{eq:euii_avg}.

\subsubsection{Comparison at Common Error Rates \note{TO DO}}
\label{subsubsec:seq_calibration}
It may be interesting to compare designs that share the same error rates (T1E and Power), as done in \citet{held2025balancing}.
Since no closed form is available for the sequential operating characteristics,
both the threshold and the sample size are calibrated numerically, following the same simulation based calibration principle used by \citet{zhu2017bayesian} and \citet{shi2019control} to reproduce a target frequentist T1E in a Bayesian sequential design, here extended to the avgT1E. 
\note{Here maybe would be nice to add an algorithm box}
\begin{comment}


The threshold search (Algorithm~\ref{alg:calibrate_threshold}) finds the common posterior probability $p$, applied at every look, that
drives the avgT1E to a target level, using the same seed so that avgT1E is a monotone non-increasing function of $p$. The sample size search (Algorithm~\ref{alg:calibrate_design}) wraps this threshold
search inside a search over the maximum sample size: keeping the information and allocation ratios costant, the smallest size reaching a target avgPower at a chosen $\delta$ is obtained.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{\textsc{CalibrateThreshold}: calibrate threshold $p$ for a target avgT1E}
\label{alg:calibrate_threshold}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require target $\alpha^\ast$; schedule $\{(n_{T,k}, n_{C,k})\}_{k=1}^{K}$; analysis priors $\pi_A$ (control), vague prior (treatment); design prior $\pi_D$; decision rule builder $\textsc{Builder}(\cdot)$ (default: criterion $\in \{\SC, \DC\}$ applied with threshold $p$ at every look); search interval $[p_{\text{lo}}, p_{\text{hi}}]$
\State $D_p \gets \textsc{Builder}(p)$ \Comment{a single scalar $p$ must determine the full stopping rule; other choices of \textsc{Builder} realize different boundary shapes}
\State $\text{avgT1E}(p) \gets$ Monte Carlo estimate of $\Pr(\text{success} \mid \delta = 0, \pi_D)$ under $D_p$ \Comment{common random numbers across $p$}
\State $p^\ast \gets$ root of $\text{avgT1E}(p) - \alpha^\ast$ on $[p_{\text{lo}}, p_{\text{hi}}]$ \Comment{avgT1E is decreasing in $p$}
\State \Return $(p^\ast, D_{p^\ast})$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}


\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{\textsc{CalibrateDesign}: joint calibration of threshold and sample size}
\label{alg:calibrate_design}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require target avgT1E $\alpha^\ast$; target avgPower $1-\beta^\ast$ at $\delta^\ast$; base schedule $\{(n_{T,k}^{0}, n_{C,k}^{0})\}_{k=1}^{K}$; $\pi_A$, $\pi_D$; criterion
\State $r \gets n_{T,K}^{0} / n_{C,K}^{0}$ \Comment{allocation ratio}
\State $t_k \gets n_{C,k}^{0} / n_{C,K}^{0}$ for $k = 1,\dots,K$ \Comment{information ratio}
\Function{Schedule}{$n$}
    \State $n_{C,k} \gets \operatorname{round}(t_k \, n)$, enforced strictly increasing in $k$
    \State $n_{T,k} \gets \operatorname{round}(r \, n_{C,k})$
    \State \Return $\{(n_{T,k}, n_{C,k})\}$
\EndFunction
\Function{EvalAt}{$n$}
    \State schedule $\gets$ \Call{Schedule}{$n$}
    \State $(p, D_p) \gets$ \Call{CalibrateThreshold}{$\alpha^\ast$, schedule, $\pi_A$, $\pi_D$, criterion}
    \State $\text{Power} \gets$ Monte Carlo estimate of avgPower$(\delta^\ast)$ under $D_p$
    \State \Return $(\text{Power}, p, D_p, \text{schedule})$
\EndFunction
\State $n \gets n_{C,K}^{0}$; $(\text{Power}, \cdot, \cdot, \cdot) \gets$ \Call{EvalAt}{$n$}
\If{$\text{Power} \geq 1-\beta^\ast$}
    \State halve $n$ repeatedly, bracketing $[\text{lo}, \text{hi}]$, until $\text{Power}(\text{lo}) < 1-\beta^\ast$
\Else
    \State double $n$ repeatedly, bracketing $[\text{lo}, \text{hi}]$, until $\text{Power}(\text{hi}) \geq 1-\beta^\ast$
\EndIf
\While{$\text{hi} - \text{lo} > 1$}
    \State $\text{mid} \gets \lfloor (\text{lo}+\text{hi})/2 \rfloor$
    \If{$\text{Power}(\text{mid}) \geq 1-\beta^\ast$}
        \State $\text{hi} \gets \text{mid}$
    \Else
        \State $\text{lo} \gets \text{mid}$
    \EndIf
\EndWhile
\State \Return \Call{EvalAt}{$\text{hi}$}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
 
\Remark The information ratio $t_k$ is computed solely from the newly enrolled control sample size $n_{C,k}$. It does not account for the effective
sample size contributed by the historical prior $\pi_A$, which is present at every look from the first one onward. When borrowing is active, this
makes $t_k$ only an approximation to the true information fraction, since the prior's fixed contribution matters proportionally more at the
earlier, smaller looks.

\Remark The decision rule builder $\textsc{Builder}(p)$ defaults to the SC or DC criterion applied at every look, a Pocock-style constant threshold:
$p_k = p$ for every look $k = 1,\dots,K$. However, it is also possible to provide other builders that are a function of a single parameter $p$,
allowing different boundary shapes to be calibrated by the same procedure. Using the information ratio $t_k$ of Algorithm~\ref{alg:calibrate_design}
(so $t_K = 1$), examples include an O'Brien Fleming shape, $p_k = \Phi\!\left(\Phi^{-1}(p)/\sqrt{t_k}\right)$, the asymptotic transform of the
classical boundary derived by \citet{shi2019control}, strict early and relaxing down to $p$ at the final look. A power family sets $p_k = 1 - (1-p)\,t_k^{\rho}$
for a shape exponent $\rho$. Haybittle-Peto sets $p_k = p_{\text{HP}}$ (e.g.\ $0.999$) for every interim $k < K$ and $p_K = p$ at the final look. These
four shapes mirror the O'Brien Fleming, power, and constant threshold spending functions that \citet{zhu2017bayesian} compare in a Bayesian sequential
design with a Normal endpoint, calibrated here by the same threshold search rather than a separately derived spending schedule. A builder can also add
a residual correction on top of such a shape, as in \citet{shi2019control}, pushing the asymptotic boundary toward certainty by an amount controlled
by a second parameter, refining an asymptotically valid boundary into one with exact finite sample control. Any of these shapes can also be combined
with a futility rule by supplying a builder that returns a non-null futility criterion at the relevant looks, so that the threshold search calibrates
the design taking the futility stopping into account.
\end{comment}


\subsection{Borrowing on the Treatment Contrast}
\label{subsec:contrast_limitation}

All the metrics developed above (average T1E, average power, and the EUII) assume borrowing on the control arm. Historical information could instead be placed directly on the treatment contrast $\delta$. We restrict the present work to borrowing on the control arm because the metrics do not transfer cleanly to that setting. We outline the three obstacles below.

\paragraph{The Prior Conflicts with the Null}
A prior placed on $\delta$ typically favours a beneficial effect, which directly conflicts with the null hypothesis. This conflict inflates the classical T1E and it has been shown that requiring strict frequentist control of it forces the historical information to be
 discarded altogether \citep{kopp-schneider_power_2020, psioda_bayesian_2019}.

\paragraph{No Average T1E Analogue}
The results of this manuscript rely on the fact that setting the analysis prior equal to the design prior allow exact control of the avgT1E when borrowing on the control arm. \citet{psioda_bayesian_2019} apply the same idea to contrast borrowing, integrating the conditional rejection probability over a null design prior,
defined as the analysis prior truncated and normalized to $\delta \le \delta_{\text{null}}$. This can be calibrated to a target level, but only when that null prior places sufficient mass near $\delta_{\text{null}}$. Nonetheless, \citet{best_beyond_2025} show that no exact analogue of the average T1E result is available
for contrast borrowing.

\paragraph{The EUII Cannot Be Built}
In place of the T1E, \citet{best_beyond_2025} propose a joint probability metric:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:t3e}
  \int_{\delta \le \delta_{\text{null}}} \Pr(\text{Success}\mid\delta)\, p(\delta)\, d\delta \;=\; \avgTIE\times\Pr(\delta \le \delta_{\text{null}}),
\end{equation}
the probability that the effect is null or harmful and the trial succeeds, also called the Type III error \citep{spiegelhalter_monitoring_1986} or pre-posterior probability of a false positive result.
The EUII is built from the diagnostic odds ratio, whose false positive input must be a conditional rate: the probability of success given that the treatment does not work, that is, the T1E.
The Type III error is instead a joint probability, and its extra factor $\Pr(\delta \le \delta_{\text{null}})$ reflects only how likely the null was assumed to be, not the trial's ability to control errors.
It therefore cannot serve as a replacement for T1E.

We leave the extension of these metrics to borrowing on the treatment contrast as a direction for future work.


\section{Applications}
\label{sec:Applications}
